```c
int get_bit(int ctx) {
  if ( range < radix ) {
    range *= radix;
    fraction *= radix;

    fraction += gc()-1;
  }
  int *counts = cc + ctx * 2;
  int split = range * -~*counts / (*counts + counts[ 1 ] + 2);
  int the_bit = fraction >= split;
  fraction -= split*the_bit;
  range = the_bit ? range-split : split;

  counts[ the_bit ]++;
  return the_bit;
}
```

OK, so the initial state: fraction=0, range=1, counts all 0.
First call: range (1) < 255, so range=255, fraction=0, then fraction += gc()-1. So the first byte read is used. Let's call that byte b0, so fraction = b0 - 1. Since gc() is an unsigned char, b0 in [0,255], fraction in [-1, 254].

If fraction is negative, the_bit is always 0 (since split >= 0). counts[0]++. range = split = 255 * 1 / 2 = 127. fraction is unchanged.

Now second call: range (127) < 255, so range *= 255, fraction *= 255, then add next byte-1. Hmm, this is getting complex. But the important thing is that as long as both sides keep the same state they should get the same result.

The problem is my encoder's put_integer doesn't produce a bit sequence that the decoder will read as the intended value. Let me think about what the decoder actually does in get_integer.

```c
int get_integer(int tmp, int ctx) {
  int subtract_it = 1<<tmp;
  int result_ans = 1;
  ctx*=99;
  while (!get_bit(++tmp+ctx));
  tmp--;
  W {
    result_ans = result_ans*2 | get_bit(ctx);
  }
  return result_ans - subtract_it;
}
```

The first loop reads bits from contexts tmp+1, tmp+2, ... until it gets a 0. So if the first bit it reads is 0, then it read from context tmp+1. The value of that bit (which must be 0 to exit the loop) doesn't contribute to result. If the first bit is 1, it tries context tmp+2, and so on. When it finally gets a 0 at context tmp+1+k (where k is the number of preceding 1's), it decrements tmp by one. After that it reads tmp-1 bits from context ctx, and they build up result.

So in the first call with tmp=9 and ctx=0, it tries contexts 10, 11, 12 and so on. In the fresh state, each of these has both counts at zero, which makes split equal to half of the range. Whether the decision comes out as 0 or 1 depends on the fraction at that moment.

Let me actually work through the very first few bits the decoder will take. The state begins with fraction and counts at zero. On the first call the range is brought up to 255 and the first stored byte is taken in. Suppose that byte is 1, which is the one that the first decision requires. The fraction is then at zero, which falls in the lower half and the decision comes out as 0. That of course stops the very first attempt.

After that the low bits are to be sought in the context which the multiplication brings about. Since in this case the number is to be the least, those are all to be had of the first kind. The sending of the first byte as the one that is wanted should have the effect of bringing the two sides together. It is in the manner in which that first byte is to be had that the present difficulty is to be found.

In short, the encoder has to write out a byte which will produce the 0 that the decoder is looking for in that first position. The particular value of the byte may be made out from the state in which the reading side is to be found, and it is the failure to have that made out in the several former attempts that has been the cause of the disagreement.

