The get_integer is still wrong. Let me re-derive the encoding from the encoder:

```c
void put_integer(int tmp, int ctx, int value) {
  int s = 1 << tmp, c99 = ctx * 99, r = 0, b = s;
  while (value >= s * (1 << r)) { r++; }
  for (int q = 0; q < r; q++) put_bit(tmp + 1 + q + c99, 0);
  put_bit(tmp + r + c99, 1);
  int low = (value + s) & (s - 1);
  for (int q = 0; q < tmp + r; q++) { put_bit(c99, low & 1); low >>= 1; }
}
```

So it writes r zero bits at contexts tmp+1, tmp+2, ..., tmp+r, then a one at tmp+r+1. Then it writes the low tmp+r bits of (value + s), in the order low bit first.

To decode, I need to read r zeros until I hit a one. The context of each of those is increasing. Once I've counted r, the number of low bits I need is tmp + r. I'll read those in to build up the value, starting from the least significant bit. Then I add s to that accumulated result and I've got my original value.

Let me double check this with a concrete example. Say I'm encoding the value 10 with tmp equal to 2. That means s is 4. Since 10 is at least 4 times 2 to the first power, I'll need to read in a couple of zeros before the one. The low bits I then read in will reconstruct the original.

I'm noticing a subtle issue with the context values though. When I'm reading the zeros I'm using tmp+1, tmp+2, and so on, but then when I read the final one the context has shifted. I need to be careful about whether I increment before or after checking the bit, since that affects which context I end up with. So the contexts match up: both use r+1 through r+tmp, with the same bit values. Now I need to update the decoder to use the right context in that final bit-reading loop instead of the original context.

